'They are of the nature of arising and passing away;
having arisen, they cease;
their pacification is happiness' —

From the wholesome preparatory in <s>Kāma</s>, ten; from eight, that differently;
from the wholesome, nine; from eleven, that; seven from the afflicted, separately;
from fourteen, those eight; from the path-of-deportment and maturation-born,
those from seven; six from the craft-based; that from seven;
from the creation [mind], two; from two, that same; in the Form [Realm], from the wholesome preparatory,
twelve; that from ten; from the wholesome, eight; from the effortless,
that from five; nine from the afflicted; that from eleven, again;
from the maturation-born and path-of-deportment, seven; that from five; two
two from the superknowledge-fruit mind; from two only, likewise that;
in the Formless, from the effortful, seven; from the wholesome, from six only, that;
from the effortless wholesome, seven; from four, that again is remembered;
from the afflicted, eight; from ten, that; six minds from the maturation-born;
from four, that; six from the learner; from four, again that same.
From the non-learner, five; that too from five — as immediately preceding [condition].

'Because of having applied thought and sustained thought, in the desire-realm wholesome mind;
regret, torpor, and unwholesome [states] are absent in the first [dhyāna]; and from that [point],
in the inter-dhyāna, applied thought also, and sustained thought also, beyond that' —
